\description{
Compute the design-based estimate of the finite-population variance for one
or more numeric variables in a survey design, with optional grouping,
uncertainty quantification, and metadata-driven labelling. Matches
\code{survey::svyvar()} numerically (Kish \code{n/(n-1)} correction) on Taylor,
replicate, twophase, and nonprob designs.
}
\details{
Confidence intervals use the normal-Wald approximation on the SE of the
variance estimate: \code{ci_low = variance - z * se},
\code{ci_high = variance + z * se}, where \code{z = qnorm((1 + conf_level) / 2)}.
The bounds are \strong{not clamped}. When the true variance is near zero with
wide SE, \code{ci_low} may be negative. Users who want non-negative lower
bounds can clamp at \code{0} post-hoc. This behaviour matches
\code{survey::svyvar()}.

Under \code{na_handling = "pairwise"} (the default), each focal variable
contributes its own per-variable complete-case count to \code{n}. Under
\code{na_handling = "listwise"}, every output row shares the intersection
complete-case count — rows with \code{NA} in any selected variable are
excluded from every variable's calculation.
}
